    [BugFix][Compilation] Infer local workers for external DP static kernels (#18720)
    
    ### What this PR does / why we need it?
    
    In external DP mode, each independently launched serving instance owns
    one DP replica. With TP1, `local_world_size * data_parallel_size_local`
    therefore returns 1 even when eight workers share a physical node. The
    static kernel backend then creates a separate compilation group for
    every worker, which can start eight compilation pools on the same node
    and increase host memory pressure.
    
    Infer the local worker count from the initialized full DP worker world
    and vLLM's cached node count in external DP mode. Preserve the internal
    DP calculation and explicit `LOCAL_WORLD_SIZE` overrides. Check that the
    runtime world covers all DP workers and is divisible by the node count.
    This reuses cached topology without adding distributed collectives or a
    fixed device count.
    
    The calculation assumes equal worker counts per node and contiguous
    ranks on each node, matching the static kernel backend's grouping
    requirements.
    
    ### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?
    
    External DP launches can share one static compilation group per physical
    node without manually exporting `LOCAL_WORLD_SIZE`. For example, eight
    workers on one node resolve to 8, and 32 workers on four nodes resolve
    to 8. Internal DP launches, explicit overrides, and configurations with
    static kernels disabled retain their existing behavior.
    
    ### How was this patch tested?
    
    - Cold startup of eight independent serving instances on one
    eight-device Ascend node: DP8/TP1/EP8, one visible device per instance,
    and no preset `LOCAL_WORLD_SIZE`. MMC DRAM was configured as 40GB with
    device_count=8; GPU memory utilization was 0.90 and max_model_len was
    135000.
    - The backend logged `runtime_world_size=8, node_count=1` and set
    `LOCAL_WORLD_SIZE=8`. Only rank 0 ran `op_compiler -j384`; the
    simultaneous compilation group count peaked at 1. All 883 operator JSONs
    across six batches compiled successfully and produced six new packages.
    - All eight workers completed graph capture. All eight health and model
    endpoints returned HTTP 200. Startup completed in 776.8 seconds. Sampled
    total host memory usage peaked at 871.25 GiB, with at least 634.14 GiB
    available; all 54 SSH probes succeeded. No startup errors, direct
    reclaim, allocation stalls, or new OOM records were observed. Total host
    memory includes the model, MMC, compilation, and system usage.
    
    Real four-node DP32 startup has not been tested with this patch. The
    single-node startup validates the compilation grouping change; it does
    not establish the resource requirements of a four-node deployment.
    
    
